{
  "version": 1,
  "cells": [
    {
      "id": "nb19-c01",
      "kind": "markdown",
      "source": "# 19 — HTTP Services & Clients\n\nBrew & Bytes is franchising, and the franchise needs an **online\nordering API**: the website, the mobile app, and every partner\ndelivery service all speak HTTP to the same backend. This notebook\nteaches how ARO serves HTTP — and how it calls other people's APIs.\n\nARO's HTTP story is **contract-first**: routes live in an\n`openapi.yaml` file, not in code. A feature set handles a route when\nits *name* equals the route's `operationId`. No contract file — no\nserver, no open port. The contract cannot drift from the code because\nthe contract *is* the router.\n\nA notebook session has no running server and makes no network calls —\nthe validator keeps these cells deterministic. So we do what serving\ncode does, with literal stand-ins for real requests: extract, decide,\nrespond. Everything that needs a port or a network is shown in\nannotated code blocks, with the shipped examples\n(`Examples/HelloWorldAPI`, `Examples/UserService`) as the running\ncounterparts.",
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "id": "nb19-c02",
      "kind": "code",
      "source": "(listMenu: Online Ordering API) {\n    Retrieve the <menu-items> from the <web-menu-repository>.\n    Return an <OK: status> with <menu-items>.\n}",
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "id": "nb19-c03",
      "kind": "markdown",
      "source": "## What just happened\n\nThe cell answered `Defined (listMenu: Online Ordering API)` — a\ndefinition *registers*; nothing ran. Three things make this an HTTP\nhandler:\n\n- **The name `listMenu`** is not decoration. It must equal an\n  `operationId` in the application's `openapi.yaml`. When a request\n  matches that operation's path and method, this feature set runs:\n\n  ```yaml\n  paths:\n    /menu:\n      get:\n        operationId: listMenu    # <- the feature set name\n  ```\n\n- **`Retrieve … from the <web-menu-repository>.`** is a *REQUEST*\n  action — it pulls data into the handler's scope. In a running shop\n  the repository would be seeded from a `.store` file or earlier\n  requests.\n- **`Return an <OK: status> with <menu-items>.`** is the *RESPONSE*.\n  `OK` maps to HTTP `200`; the payload is serialized as JSON and\n  becomes the response body.\n\nThe routing chain is: request arrives → path + method matched against\nthe contract → `operationId` looked up → feature set with that name\nexecutes. A request that matches no path gets `404`; a known path with\nan undefined method gets `405`; an operation with no matching feature\nset gets `501 Not Implemented` — all generated by the runtime, none\nwritten by you\n([Chapter 17](../Book/TheLanguageGuide/Chapter17-OpenAPI.md)).",
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "id": "nb19-c04",
      "kind": "markdown",
      "source": "## Variants: the request context\n\nInside a handler, the request is reachable through context\nidentifiers — each one a REQUEST-side Extract away:\n\n| Identifier | Carries | Example |\n|------------|---------|---------|\n| `<pathParameters: id>` | values from the URL template `/orders/{id}` | `Extract the <id> from the <pathParameters: id>.` |\n| `<queryParameters: limit>` | `?limit=10` pairs | `Extract the <limit> from the <queryParameters: limit>.` |\n| `<request: body>` | the parsed request body | `Extract the <data> from the <request: body>.` |\n| `<headers: authorization>` | HTTP headers | `Extract the <token> from the <headers: authorization>.` |\n\nA session has no live request, so we build one as a plain record and\nextract from it exactly the way a handler would. The shapes are\nidentical — that is the point.",
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "id": "nb19-c05",
      "kind": "code",
      "source": "Create the <sim-request> with {\n    method: \"POST\",\n    path: \"/orders\",\n    pathParameters: { id: \"A-1001\" },\n    body: { customer: \"Ada\", item: \"flat white\", qty: 2 }\n}.\nExtract the <sim-params> from the <sim-request: pathParameters>.\nExtract the <sim-order-id> from the <sim-params: id>.\nExtract the <sim-body> from the <sim-request: body>.\nExtract the <sim-item> from the <sim-body: item>.\nExtract the <sim-qty> from the <sim-body: qty>.\nLog \"Order ${sim-order-id}: ${sim-qty} x ${sim-item}\" to the <console>.",
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "id": "nb19-c06",
      "kind": "code",
      "source": "Create the <unit-price> with 4.2.\nCompute the <line-total> from <sim-qty> * <unit-price>.\nReturn a <Created: status> with { order: <sim-order-id>, item: <sim-item>, total: <line-total> }.",
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "id": "nb19-c07",
      "kind": "markdown",
      "source": "`Return` in a session displays its payload — exactly what the wire\nwould carry as the response body. The status qualifier picks the HTTP\nstatus code:\n\n| Qualifier | HTTP | Typical use |\n|-----------|------|-------------|\n| `OK` | 200 | successful read |\n| `Created` | 201 | resource created (POST) |\n| `Accepted` | 202 | work deferred |\n| `NoContent` | 204 | success, nothing to say (DELETE) |\n| `BadRequest` | 400 | the client's data is wrong |\n| `NotFound` | 404 | no such resource |\n\nAnd the **happy path holds**\n([ARO-0006](../Proposals/ARO-0006-error-philosophy.md)): when a\nstatement fails — the order isn't in the repository, the body lacks a\nfield — the runtime maps the failure to an error response (`404`,\n`400`, `500`) with the failing statement's plain-language message as\nthe body. You write no error branches.\n\nThe most common handler shape is a **keyed lookup**: path parameter\nin, `where` clause against a repository, entity out.",
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "id": "nb19-c08",
      "kind": "code",
      "source": "Create the <seed-order> with { id: \"A-1001\", customer: \"Ada\", total: 8.4 }.\nStore the <seed-order> into the <web-order-repository>.\nRetrieve the <found-order> from the <web-order-repository> where <id> is \"A-1001\".\nExtract the <found-total> from the <found-order: total>.\nLog \"Order A-1001 total: ${found-total}\" to the <console>.",
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "id": "nb19-c09",
      "kind": "code",
      "source": "(getOrder: Online Ordering API) {\n    Extract the <order-id> from the <pathParameters: id>.\n    Retrieve the <order> from the <web-order-repository> where <id> is <order-id>.\n    Return an <OK: status> with <order>.\n}",
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "id": "nb19-c10",
      "kind": "markdown",
      "source": "## Walkthrough: the smallest complete service\n\n`Examples/HelloWorldAPI` is a whole HTTP service in three small files.\nThe contract declares one route:\n\n```yaml\n# openapi.yaml\npaths:\n  /hello:\n    get:\n      operationId: sayHello\n```\n\nThe entry point starts the server and stays alive:\n\n```aro\n(* main.aro *)\n(Application-Start: Hello World API) {\n    Log \"Hello World API starting...\" to the <console>.\n    Start the <http-server> with <contract>.\n    Keepalive the <application> for the <events>.\n    Return an <OK: status> for the <startup>.\n}\n```\n\nAnd the handler is two statements:\n\n```aro\n(* hello.aro *)\n(sayHello: Hello API) {\n    Create the <message> with \"Hello World\".\n    Return an <OK: status> with { message: <message> }.\n}\n```\n\n`Start the <http-server>` and `Keepalive` are exactly the things a\nnotebook cell must not do — they bind a port and block for events —\nwhich is why serving lives in fenced blocks here. Run it for real\nwith `aro run Examples/HelloWorldAPI`, then\n`curl http://localhost:8000/hello`.",
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "id": "nb19-c11",
      "kind": "markdown",
      "source": "## Walkthrough: a real CRUD service\n\n`Examples/UserService` is the full pattern: one contract, five\noperations, three source files (`main.aro`, `users.aro`,\n`events.aro`). All `.aro` files in the directory are discovered\nautomatically — no imports. The operation table:\n\n| Method + path | operationId | Handler shape |\n|---------------|-------------|---------------|\n| `GET /users` | `listUsers` | Retrieve all, `OK` |\n| `POST /users` | `createUser` | body → Create → Store → Emit → `Created` |\n| `GET /users/{id}` | `getUser` | path param → `where` lookup → `OK` |\n| `PUT /users/{id}` | `updateUser` | Retrieve + Merge + Store → `OK` |\n| `DELETE /users/{id}` | `deleteUser` | Delete `where` → `NoContent` |\n\nThe create handler shows how HTTP work hands off to the event bus:\n\n```aro\n(createUser: User API) {\n    Extract the <user-data> from the <request: body>.\n    Create the <user> with <user-data>.\n    Store the <stored-user: user> into the <user-repository>.\n    Emit a <UserCreated: event> with <stored-user>.\n    Return a <Created: status> with <stored-user>.\n}\n```\n\nThe HTTP response goes out immediately; a `UserCreated Handler` in\n`events.aro` writes the user to a file *after* the request is done.\nThe handler never knows who listens — the decoupling you learned in\n[09 — Feature Sets & Events](09-feature-sets-and-events.repl), now\nearning rent in an API.",
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "id": "nb19-c12",
      "kind": "markdown",
      "source": "## Request bodies: streams versus values\n\nUploads are where naive servers die. ARO's rule\n([ARO-0090](../Proposals/ARO-0090-streaming-io-and-materialization.md)):\n**streams don't have a size — values do.** A handler that only *moves*\nits body (`Write` it to a file, `Return` it, `Send` it on) never\nbuilds it in memory:\n\n```aro\n(uploadDocument: Files API) {\n    Extract the <upload> from the <request: body>.   (* binds - reads nothing *)\n    Write the <upload> to the <file: target>.        (* streams; 4 GB is fine *)\n    Return a <Created: status> with <name>.\n}\n```\n\nA handler that *reads* the body — a field access, `Compute`, `Store`,\na `when` guard — turns it into a value, and values are bounded. The\nbound is declared per route in the contract, default 1 MB:\n\n```yaml\npaths:\n  /orders/import:\n    post:\n      operationId: importOrders\n      x-aro-max-body: 256KB\n```\n\nOver the limit, the client gets `413` *before* the body is read.\nWhich category each route falls into is computed from your source\nbefore the server binds a port — `aro check` reports it per route.\nDetails: [Chapter 50](../Book/TheLanguageGuide/Chapter50-RequestBodies.md).",
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "id": "nb19-c13",
      "kind": "markdown",
      "source": "## The other direction: the HTTP client\n\nThe ordering API is also a *consumer* — card payments, delivery\ntracking, a weather widget for the terrace. The `Request` action is\nthe client side, and the preposition picks the method:\n\n```aro\n(* GET - preposition 'from' *)\nCreate the <api-url> with \"https://api.example.com/menu\".\nRequest the <response> from the <api-url>.\nExtract the <payload> from the <response: body>.\n\n(* POST - preposition 'to', payload via 'with' *)\nRequest the <receipt> to the <payments-url> with <charge-data>.\n\n(* PUT / DELETE / PATCH - explicit 'via' *)\nRequest the <update-result> via PUT the <order-url> with <changes>.\nRequest the <deletion> via DELETE the <order-url>.\n\n(* Full control: a config object *)\nRequest the <tracked> from the <courier-url> with {\n    headers: { \"Authorization\": \"Bearer token\" },\n    timeout: 10\n}.\n```\n\nThe response carries the parsed body plus metadata:\n`<response: statusCode>`, `<response: headers>`,\n`<response: isSuccess>`. JSON bodies arrive already parsed.\n\nNotebook cells make no network calls, so these stay fenced.\n`Examples/WeatherClient` is the four-line running version — fetch,\nextract body, log — and `Examples/HTTPClient` adds the config-object\nvariants. Both point their URL at a local stub so tests never depend\non a third party: a habit worth stealing.\nFull reference: [Chapter 38](../Book/TheLanguageGuide/Chapter38-HTTPClient.md).",
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "id": "nb19-c14",
      "kind": "markdown",
      "source": "## Discussion\n\nWhy contract-first, when every other framework does routes-in-code?\n\n1. **The documentation cannot lie.** The spec *is* the router. If the\n   contract declares an operation with no handler, clients get an\n   honest `501`; a handler without an operation never receives\n   traffic. There is no second source of truth to keep synchronized.\n2. **The contract is a working artifact.** The same `openapi.yaml`\n   drives Swagger UI, client-library generators, and mock servers —\n   the partner integrating with your ordering API starts before your\n   handlers exist.\n3. **Handlers stay legible.** Extract, process, return — every\n   handler reads the same\n   ([Chapter 19](../Book/TheLanguageGuide/Chapter19-RequestResponse.md)).\n   The café owner can read `getOrder` aloud.\n\nThe trade-off is ceremony: a one-route toy needs a YAML file. ARO\naccepts that cost deliberately — an undocumented API is a bug the\nframework refuses to let you write.\n\nOne habit matters above all: **extract early, return precisely.**\nPull everything you need from the request in the first statements;\nchoose the status qualifier that tells the client the truth.",
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "id": "nb19-c15",
      "kind": "markdown",
      "source": "## Real-world example: the pricing gate\n\nEvery serious API validates before it commits. The franchise caps\nonline orders at 12 cups — above that, call the shop. We package that\ndecision as a **user-defined action**\n([10 — User-Defined Actions](10-user-defined-actions.repl)) so the\n`createOrder` handler, the till, and this notebook all share one\nimplementation — and so we can exercise it *right here*, like a unit\ntest, without a server.\n\nNote the shape: a guarded early `Return` answers the bad case; when\nthe guard fails, the statement is skipped and execution continues to\nthe happy return.",
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "id": "nb19-c16",
      "kind": "code",
      "source": "(PriceOrder: Action takes <quantity>) {\n    Extract the <q> from the <input: quantity>.\n    Return a <BadRequest: status> with { error: \"Order cap is 12 cups - call the shop.\" } when <q> > 12.\n    Compute the <t> from <q> * 4.2.\n    Return an <OK: status> with { total: <t> }.\n}",
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "id": "nb19-c17",
      "kind": "code",
      "source": "Application.PriceOrder the <small-answer> from 2.\nLog <small-answer> to the <console>.\nApplication.PriceOrder the <big-answer> from 14.\nLog <big-answer> to the <console>.",
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "id": "nb19-c18",
      "kind": "markdown",
      "source": "Two calls, two verdicts: the small order comes back with\n`status: OK` and a total; the oversized one with `status: BadRequest`\nand the reason. In the running service, `createOrder` calls the same\naction and forwards the verdict — the HTTP layer stays a thin skin\nover decisions you can test in isolation.\n\nThis is why the pattern matters beyond the café: a payment provider\nanswers `402` with a machine-readable reason; an airline API rejects\nan overbooked seat with `409`; a rate-limited partner answers `429`\nwith a `Retry-After` header. In each case the *decision* is business\nlogic and the *status code* is its honest translation — HTTP as a\nshared vocabulary between systems that have never met. Contract-first\nplus status discipline is what lets a delivery platform integrate\nagainst your ordering API from the YAML alone.\n\n## Related\n\n- Next: [20 — WebSockets & Real-Time](20-websockets-and-realtime.repl)\n- Builds on: [09 — Feature Sets & Events](09-feature-sets-and-events.repl),\n  [10 — User-Defined Actions](10-user-defined-actions.repl),\n  [13 — Repositories & State](13-repositories-and-state.repl),\n  [14 — Building Applications](14-building-applications.repl)\n- Language Guide: [Chapter 17 — OpenAPI](../Book/TheLanguageGuide/Chapter17-OpenAPI.md),\n  [Chapter 18 — HTTP Feature Sets](../Book/TheLanguageGuide/Chapter18-HTTPFeatureSets.md),\n  [Chapter 19 — Request/Response Patterns](../Book/TheLanguageGuide/Chapter19-RequestResponse.md),\n  [Chapter 38 — HTTP Client](../Book/TheLanguageGuide/Chapter38-HTTPClient.md),\n  [Chapter 50 — Request Bodies](../Book/TheLanguageGuide/Chapter50-RequestBodies.md)\n- Wiki: [Guide — HTTP Services](https://github.com/arolang/aro/wiki/Guide-HTTP-Services)\n- Specification: [ARO-0008 — I/O Services](../Proposals/ARO-0008-io-services.md),\n  [ARO-0090 — Streaming I/O](../Proposals/ARO-0090-streaming-io-and-materialization.md)\n- Run the real thing: `aro run Examples/HelloWorldAPI`,\n  `aro run Examples/UserService`, `aro run Examples/WeatherClient`",
      "outputs": []
    }
  ]
}
